# Workers Assets reads this file from the assets root and does not serve it.
#
# The discovery document is a kill switch, and a kill switch behind an edge
# cache nobody set is a kill switch with an unknown delay. `no-cache` is "hold
# this, but ask before using it" rather than "do not store it" -- the same
# choice, for the same reason, that the API makes on every answer carrying a
# Version. Without it the outage message a human just wrote reaches people
# whenever Cloudflare's heuristics decide it should.
/discovery.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The two installers, held to the same rule and for the same reason. A broken
# installer is the same category of artifact as a stale kill switch: the fix has
# to reach people when it is written, not when Cloudflare's heuristics decide.
# These are also the only files here a person pipes straight into a shell, so
# "the copy you fetched is the copy we published" is worth more than the cache.
#
# Content-Type is stated rather than sniffed. `irm | iex` hands PowerShell
# whatever the response says it is, and an octet-stream body can arrive as bytes
# instead of the string `iex` needs.
/install.sh
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Content-Type: text/x-shellscript; charset=utf-8
/install.ps1
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# The two webfonts, and the rule inverts here. Everything above is `no-cache`
# because it is a thing a human edits to reach people immediately -- a kill
# switch, or a script somebody is about to pipe into a shell. A font changes
# only when the subset is regenerated and committed, which is roughly never, and
# caching it is what stops `font-display: block` costing a blank first frame on
# every repeat visit.
#
# A week rather than the year an `immutable` asset would get, because these
# filenames carry no content hash. `/fonts/monaspace-neon.woff2` is written
# literally in globals.css and layout.tsx, so a regenerated subset ships under
# the same URL and there is nothing to bust the cache with. `immutable` would be
# promising something this arrangement cannot keep: widen the whitelist to fix a
# missing glyph and a returning visitor would keep the sheared copy until the
# entry expired.
#
# A week is the staleness anyone can actually be handed, and the request it
# still saves is every repeat visit inside one. Content-hashed names are the
# real fix and would earn the year back; they are more moving parts than this
# needs while the fonts change once a release at most.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
